UPDATE public.news_articles SET content = $boi_t$LEGO.com has just unveiled a new seasonal gift-with-purchase, the [40900](/sets/40900-scary-haunted-tree) Scary Haunted Tree, and it’s looking appropriately spooky. This 218-piece Creator set promises to add some Halloween flair to your collection, featuring a black tree with posable arms and a mouth that lights up when you press a button. It also comes with a small pumpkin and a crow build, making it a nice little diorama piece for the spooky season.

The set is designed for builders aged 8 and up, and the press release highlights its potential as both a fun building activity and a decorative item. The tree itself stands over 6 inches tall, 3 inches long, and 7 inches wide, offering a decent display presence for its implied size as a GWP. The light-up feature adds a nice interactive element, perfect for creating eerie scenes or just spooky ambient lighting for your desk.

LEGO hasn't confirmed the dates or the qualifying spend. Reports put the threshold at around US$100 / £90 / €100 on LEGO.com and the start at 21 September. It follows two earlier Halloween gifts with purchase that also lit up: [40772](/sets/40772-celebration-series-luminous-ghost) Luminous Ghost (2025) and [40697](/sets/40697-halloween-pumpkin) Halloween Pumpkin (2024).

The comments section on Brickset shows a mix of reactions, with some users finding it "very cute" and comparing it to previous seasonal GWPs like last year's ghost set. Others are already making humorous comparisons to pop culture characters. The consensus is that it’s a charming addition for Halloween fans, especially with the light-up function.

40900 is a gift with purchase: it isn't sold separately, so it has no retail price in India or anywhere else. It comes free with a qualifying LEGO order while the promotion runs; LEGO hasn't confirmed the qualifying spend (reports put it at around US$100 / £90 / €100). No Indian retailer has announced it.

If you want one, the official route is a qualifying LEGO order during the promotion. Outside it, the only option is the secondary market.

Verdict: IMPORT ONLY. Not in Indian stores — grey market or wait.

Corrected 29 Sep 2026: an earlier version gave an estimated import price of ₹3,400, calculated from a US retail price of $24.99. 40900 is a gift with purchase and has no retail price, so those figures are removed. This page now also carries our 22 Sep report on the same reveal, whose address redirects here.

On that bombshell, bubyee.$boi_t$
