UPDATE public.news_articles SET content = $boi_t$This week's LEGO set popularity charts have thrown a curveball, and it's not a giant, soulless space station or a ridiculously oversized vehicle. No, the surprise standout is the LEGO [40900](/sets/40900-scary-haunted-tree) Scary Haunted Tree, a set that has apparently captured the imagination of builders everywhere this past week. While the usual suspects like the Burj Khalifa and some older fan-favourites popped up, it's this spooky little tree that's earned a spot in the top viewed sets on Brickset.

The list, compiled from set detail page views, shows the Scary Haunted Tree climbing the ranks. It's a curious phenomenon, especially for a set that isn't part of a major ongoing theme or a highly anticipated new release. The other sets making waves include the Burj Khalifa ([21055](/sets/21055-burj-khalifa)), Aquabase Invasion ([7775](/sets/7775-aquabase-invasion)), Yoda Bust ([75255](/sets/75255-yoda)), Black Knight's Castle ([6086](/sets/6086-black-knights-castle)), and the Lion Knights' Castle ([10305](/sets/10305-lion-knights-castle)). It’s interesting to see older sets like Aquabase Invasion and even the rather obscure [75201](/sets/75201-first-order-at-st) First Order AT-ST resurfacing in the top viewed. Some fans on Reddit are speculating that these older sets are appearing due to discussions on forums about "worst sets ever" or nostalgic deep dives, which makes a certain kind of sense.

The likely reason is simple: the week before, LEGO revealed 40900 as its Halloween gift with purchase, a 218-piece Creator build with a light-up mouth, a crow and a pumpkin (see our report: [LEGO Creator 40900 Scary Haunted Tree revealed](/news/lego-creator-40900-scary-haunted-tree-revealed-as-new-gift-w)).

As a gift with purchase, 40900 isn't sold separately, so no Indian retailer lists it.

40900 is a gift with purchase: it isn't sold separately, so it has no retail price in India or anywhere else. It comes free with a qualifying LEGO order while the promotion runs; LEGO hasn't confirmed the qualifying spend (reports put it at around US$100 / £90 / €100). No Indian retailer has announced it.

Outside the promotion, the only way to get one is the secondary market, or a qualifying LEGO order while travelling abroad.

Verdict: IMPORT ONLY. Not in Indian stores — grey market or wait.

Corrected 29 Sep 2026: this article said there were no official details on 40900's release, theme or price, and estimated an import price of ₹2,700 from a US price of $19.99. LEGO had already revealed 40900 as a gift with purchase, which isn't sold separately; the claim and the price are removed, and a stray second verdict line is deleted.

